!
! gasdata.inp -- critical-point data and molecular properties for the BELFEM gas models
!
!------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
! FORMAT
!------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
!
! Fixed column. Offsets are 0-based and must be preserved; they are read in
! cl_GT_InputData.cpp.
!
!   label     0..11   must equal the thermo.inp species label (max 11 characters)
!   name     12..71
!   CAS      75..90
!   M        92..101  g/mol
!   T_crit  102..110  K
!   p_crit  112..119  bar
!   Z_crit  121..128
!   omega   129..135
!   dipole  138..145  debye
!
! Everything from column 147 onward is provenance and is IGNORED by the reader. Sources
! are given per field, because a row may draw its values from more than one place:
!
!   src(M,T_crit,p_crit,Z_crit) 147..182
!   src(omega)                 185..220
!   src(dipole)                223..EOL
!
! Source tokens contain no spaces. A DOI is used wherever one exists. Other tokens:
!
!   symmetry            dipole is exactly zero by molecular point group; not a measurement
!   simple-fluid        omega is zero by Pitzer's definition for a monatomic/spherical fluid
!   estimate-from-<X>   value assumed equal to species X; an estimate, not a measurement
!   <author>-<year>     a printed source without a DOI
!   A/Zc:B              fields from A except Z_crit, which is from B
!   A/pc:B              fields from A except p_crit, which is from B
!   A/Zc:Pitzer-corr    Z_crit estimated from Pitzer's correlation Zc = 0.291 - 0.08*omega,
!                       used where a critical volume is not available
!   A/Zc:computed       Z_crit computed as pc*Vc/(R*Tc) rather than tabulated
!   <src>-pseudopure    value for the species treated as a pseudo-pure component in
!                       engineering correlations, not a pure-component measurement.
!                       NO2 omega is such a case: the pure-component value is far
!                       lower (~0.06), but the pseudo-pure value is what reproduces
!                       NO2/N2O4 behaviour in mixture calculations.
!
!------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
! SOURCES AND LICENCE
!------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
!
! Critical-point data is generated programmatically from CoolProp 8.0.0
! (http://www.coolprop.org, MIT licence); Z_crit is computed as
! p_crit / ( rho_molar_crit * R * T_crit ). Species that CoolProp does not carry take
! individually cited values from the printed literature. Dipole moments are individual
! physical constants cited per record.
!
! Every value therefore traces to a named origin. Critical data comes from an
! MIT-licensed library. The remaining values come from the individual primary sources
! given in each row. A dipole marked "symmetry" is exactly zero by the molecular point
! group and an omega marked "simple-fluid" is zero by Pitzer's definition; neither is a
! measurement. Which species appear is decided by the reader: a row is usable only when
! its label matches a thermo.inp species and fits the eleven character label field.
!
!------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
! EXTENDING THIS TABLE
!------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
!
! Add rows in the same columns to extend the table for your own use. Critical constants
! for further species can be taken from any thermophysical compilation, for example
! Poling, Prausnitz and O'Connell, "The Properties of Gases and Liquids", 5th ed.
! (McGraw-Hill, 2001) or Perry's Chemical Engineers' Handbook. Only species whose label
! matches a thermo.inp entry are reachable by the code.
!
! Free atoms and open-shell radicals -- H, O, N, C, OH, CH3, S, SH, SO, ... -- carry no
! entry by design. A monatomic or radical species has no condensed phase and therefore
! no critical point; the values sometimes tabulated for "S" belong to bulk elemental
! sulfur (the S8/S2 melt), a different substance. These species are ideal-gas only,
! which is also the regime in which they actually occur. The noble gases are entered
! because they are atoms that do have a genuine condensed phase.
!
! Species still wanted: S8, which lacks critical constants entirely.
!
! N2O4 is deliberately excluded. It and NO2 are the same reactive system
! (N2O4 <-> 2 NO2), and NO2 is already entered as a pseudo-pure representative of it.
! Entering both would double count the association effect in any mixture containing
! the pair, which an equilibrium calculation will always produce. Note also that the
! commonly tabulated N2O4 critical volume refers to the dissociating mixture and gives
! Zc ~ 0.47; that value must not be combined with pure-component constants.
!
! Keep the columns aligned when editing. The reader takes each field by its byte
! offset, so a shifted column is read as the wrong quantity rather than rejected.
!
!----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
!           label                                                          CAS                      M    T_crit   p_crit  z_crit   omega  dipole   src(M,Tc,pc,Zc)                       src(omega)                            src(dipole)
!----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
